<Layout title="ETF Cost Model Methodology and Filing Source Boundaries" description="Our calculations separate assumptions from observed documents. Read the scope, assumptions and source boundaries behind ETF News Daily’s transparent educational"><article class="paper"><h1>ETF Cost Model Methodology and Filing Source Boundaries</h1><p class="lead">Our calculations separate assumptions from observed documents.</p><p>Updated October 16, 2026</p><p>Expense-ratio model: B(next) = (B + annual contribution) × (1 + return) × (1 − expense ratio).</p><p>Contributions arrive at the start of each whole year; expenses are simplified to annual deductions.</p><p>Actual fund accounting normally accrues expenses more frequently.</p><p>Total cost subtracts the modeled net balance from an otherwise identical zero-fee path.</p><p>Forgone growth can be negative in falling markets.</p><p>Ownership cost uses half the quoted spread and one commission at each end.</p><p>The fund comparison isolates annual fees and a front-end mutual fund load; taxes and distributions are zero in the current interface.</p><p>Withholding uses amount × yield, then sequential source-to-fund and fund-to-investor deductions; cumulative difference assumes fixed return drag, not individual tax advice.</p><p>Rates are user assumptions, not treaty determinations.</p><p>The SEC snapshot searches ETF across 485APOS, 485BPOS, N-1A and S-1 for October 1–16, 2026.</p><p>It retains primary documents in the first batch, not all search results.</p><p>Refresh retains the previous snapshot on failure; the historical window is fixed.</p><p>Document descriptions are not verified product names.</p><h2>Keep reading</h2><p><a href="/tools/">Explore the cost models</a> · <a href="/filings/">Inspect SEC filings</a></p><p>Official reference: <a href="https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/exchange-traded-funds-etfs">SEC Investor.gov: exchange-traded funds</a></p></article></Layout>